{"name":"Can Can","hex":"#d08a9b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"can-can","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of cancan."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/can-can"}],"entries":[{"title":"Can Can (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Can Can is an American punk rock band from Atlanta, Georgia. They were formed in 2007 by lead vocalist Patrick Aleph, guitarist Mary Collins, and drummer Josh Lamar. They released their independent debut album, All Hell, before signing to JDub Records, who released their next album, Monsters & Healers, the following year. They are known for Aleph's aggressive vocals and philosophical, Jewish-themed lyrics."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Can_Can_(band)","wikidata":"Q25056299"},{"title":"Can Can (Bad Manners song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Can Can\" is a song by the English 2-tone and ska band Bad Manners, released in June 1981 as the first single from their third album Gosh It's ... Bad Manners. It is an instrumental song, based on the music \"Galop infernal\" written by French composer Jacques Offenbach which was later adopted as the music for the dance the can-can. The arrangement was credited to Bad Manners. It peaked at number 3 for four weeks on the UK Singles Chart, becoming the band's joint biggest hit with \"Special Brew\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9d/Bad_Manners_Can_Can.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Can_Can_(Bad_Manners_song)","wikidata":"Q104869896"},{"title":"Can-can","blocks":[{"text":"The can-can is a high-energy, physically demanding dance that became a popular music-hall dance in the 1840s, continuing in popularity in French cabaret to modern days. Originally danced by couples, it is traditionally associated with a chorus line of female dancers. The main features of the dance are the vigorous manipulation of skirts and petticoats, along with high kicks, splits, and cartwheels."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/The_Can_Can_-_Flickr_-_Dance_Photographer_-_Brendan_Lally.jpg/330px-The_Can_Can_-_Flickr_-_Dance_Photographer_-_Brendan_Lally.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Can-can","wikidata":"Q333009"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Georges_Seurat%2C_1889-90%2C_Le_Chahut%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_170_x_141_cm%2C_Kr%C3%B6ller-M%C3%BCller_Museum.jpeg/500px-Georges_Seurat%2C_1889-90%2C_Le_Chahut%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_170_x_141_cm%2C_Kr%C3%B6ller-M%C3%BCller_Museum.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Georges_Seurat%2C_1889-90%2C_Le_Chahut%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_170_x_141_cm%2C_Kr%C3%B6ller-M%C3%BCller_Museum.jpeg/500px-Georges_Seurat%2C_1889-90%2C_Le_Chahut%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_170_x_141_cm%2C_Kr%C3%B6ller-M%C3%BCller_Museum.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Georges Seurat, 1889–90, Le Chahut, oil on canvas, 170 cm × 141 cm (67 in × 56 in), Kröller-Müller Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGeorges_Seurat%2C_1889-90%2C_Le_Chahut%2C_oil_on_canvas%2C_170_x_141_cm%2C_Kr%C3%B6ller-M%C3%BCller_Museum.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec_031.jpg/500px-Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec_031.jpg/1280px-Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec_031.jpg/500px-Henri_de_Toulouse-Lautrec_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=par